> nt interaction sample and applies the minimum cell size of 10. Column (8) uses the consumption proxy a
> nd is estimated on its available sample; amounts are set to zero only for explicit no-saving responses
>  with an inapplicable or missing amount, while other invalid values remain missing. Column (9) uses th
> e baseline reference-group definition but requires a minimum reference-group-year cell size of 25. Any
>  attendance equals one for individuals who attend religious services less often, at least monthly, or 
> at least weekly. Own income is log equivalised household income, except in column (8), which uses log(
> 1+equivalised consumption), computed from household income less reported annual savings. Reference inc
> ome is the log leave-one-out mean income of the reference group, except in column (5), which uses log 
> median reference income, column (7), which uses relative income rank, and column (8), which uses log l
> eave-one-out mean equivalised consumption. The baseline reference group is defined by year, gender, ag
> e group, two education groups, and federal state. Column (2) removes education from the reference-grou
> p definition; column (3) replaces education with a married/non-married indicator; column (6) controls 
> for reference-group-year inequality using the Gini coefficient. All models include individual, year, a
> nd state fixed effects and baseline controls including working hours. * \(p<0.10\), ** \(p<0.05\), ***
>  \(p<0.01\)." _n

